"""The authorization server this application hosts: who it is, what it signs with, where it stores.

Five settings have no safe default, and they are the five written here.

``issuer`` is the origin clients reach this server at: every token claims it,
and the discovery document publishes it, so change it to this deployment's own
origin before anything else. ``private_key`` takes the key text or the path of
a file holding it — the key loader reads the file itself — so
``OAUTH2_SERVER_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH`` carries a path and no key material is
written into the repository; ``OAUTH2_SERVER_PRIVATE_KEY_PASSPHRASE`` is what
that key is encrypted with, or nothing for a key that is not. ``encryption_key``
is what an authorization code and a refresh token are sealed with, read from
``OAUTH2_SERVER_ENCRYPTION_KEY`` as the base64url of 32 random bytes, which is
what ``encryption_key_type="key"`` means.

The recipe that wrote this file minted the key and generated both secrets for
this checkout; production sets the three variables to its own. No variable is
read while the application builds — a variable is read when the service
needing it is built — so the application runs, and its console with it, before
any is set; a request then reports the variable or the setting that is still
missing.

``scopes.available`` is the whole vocabulary this server issues, and
``scopes.default`` what a client registered naming none is given. The store
starts in memory, which forgets everything when the process ends: swap it for
``OrmPersistenceConfig()`` and generate the revision as soon as the clients
and the credentials have to outlive a restart.

Everything else keeps its default: an hour's access token, thirty days of
refresh token, the client-credentials, refresh-token and authorization-code
flows on, the password and implicit flows off, a proof key required of a
client holding no secret, revocation and discovery served, introspection not.
The flow for a device with no browser is off here because it owes one more
setting — where a person goes to type the user code, which is a page the
application builds: turn it on by naming ``device_code_verification_uri``.
The README's *Configure* section has the rest of the fields to change.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

from xtr_dependency_injection import configure, env
from xtr_security_oauth2_server.bundle import (
    AuthorizationServerConfig,
    InMemoryPersistenceConfig,
    OAuth2ServerConfig,
    ScopesConfig,
)

__all__ = ["oauth2_server"]


@configure
def oauth2_server() -> OAuth2ServerConfig:
    """Issue tokens as the configured issuer, signing and sealing with the configured keys."""
    return OAuth2ServerConfig(
        authorization_server=AuthorizationServerConfig(
            issuer="https://authorization.example.com",
            private_key=env("resolve:OAUTH2_SERVER_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH"),
            private_key_passphrase=env("OAUTH2_SERVER_PRIVATE_KEY_PASSPHRASE", default=""),
            encryption_key=env("OAUTH2_SERVER_ENCRYPTION_KEY"),
            encryption_key_type="key",
            enable_device_code_grant=False,
        ),
        scopes=ScopesConfig(available=("read", "write"), default=("read",)),
        persistence=InMemoryPersistenceConfig(),
    )
